Presidential elections were held in [[Senegal]] on 26 February 2012,<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20190127094147/http://www.sudonline.sn/ibrahima-fall-deroule-sa-feuille-de-route_a_3253.html "Candidature a La Presidentielle de 2012 du Senegal: Ibrahima Fall déroule sa feuille de route"]. Denise Zarour Medang, Sud Quotidien 10 June 2011.</ref><ref>[http://www.electionguide.org/country.php?ID=190 "IFES Election Guide – Country Profile: Senegal"].</ref> amidst controversy over the constitutional validity of a third term for incumbent president [[Abdoulaye Wade]]. In the [[Runoff election|runoff]] on 25 March,<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.yahoo.com/senegal-presidential-run-off-set-march-25-103210920.html;_ylt=AtrI0QEdlNMW4Iz79Jzk2n9vaA8F;_ylu=X3oDMTNlOXVxdTRqBG1pdAMEcGtnA2IyMjE3YzY0LWRmNzgtM2E0MS1hMmRiLWIyNTkwYjAwOWFiMQRwb3MDMwRzZWMDbG5fQWZyaWNhX2dhbAR2ZXIDMTFlZjk3NTAtNjZhZi0xMWUxLWJmZmMtNmViNDY4ZGMzYzU1;_ylv=3 |title=Senegal presidential run-off set for March 25 |agency=AFP |date=5 March 2012}}</ref> [[Macky Sall]] defeated the incumbent president. The 2015 documentary film ''Incorruptible'' chronicles both campaigns as well as the youth movement Y'en a Marre, which led protests against Wade's administration.

== Background ==
In July 2008, the National Assembly approved a constitutional amendment increasing the length of the presidential term to seven years, as it was prior to the [[2001 Senegalese constitutional referendum|adoption of the 2001 constitution]]. This extension didn't apply to [[Abdoulaye Wade]]'s 2007&ndash;2012 term, but Minister of Justice [[Madické Niang]] stressed on this occasion that Wade could potentially run for re-election in 2012 if he was still healthy.<ref name="afrol.com">{{cite news |title=afrol News - Senegal approves presidential term extension |url=http://www.afrol.com/articles/30051 |access-date=10 June 2023 |website=Afrol News |date=July 30, 2008<!-- article lists Wade (born in 1926) as 82 years old --> |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081209103312/http://www.afrol.com/articles/30051 |archive-date=December 9, 2008}}</ref>

The 26 February 2012 date for the election was decreed by [[President of Senegal]] [[Abdoulaye Wade]] on 23 November 2010. President Wade indicated that he would stand for his third term, set at seven years by the constitution.<ref>Drew Hinshaw. [https://web.archive.org/web/20101127011727/http://www.businessweek.com/news/2010-11-23/senegal-sets-next-presidential-election-for-feb-26-2012.html "Senegal Sets Next Presidential Election for Feb.&nbsp;26, 2012"]. ''Bloomberg BusinessWeek'', 23 November 2010.</ref><ref>[https://www.voanews.com/a/a-13-2009-09-17-voa50-68709547/409461.html "Senegal's President Says He'll Run in 2012"]. ''Voice of America''. 17 November 2009.</ref> While the [[2001 Senegalese constitutional referendum|2001 constitution]] limits a president to two terms, Wade argued that his [[2000 Senegalese presidential election|2000 election]] to his first seven-year term falls under the previous constitution, which did not provide for [[term limit]]s.<ref>Diadie Ba. [https://web.archive.org/web/20120920010516/http://af.reuters.com/article/topNews/idAFJOE67N0I420100824 "Senegalese leader in row over 2012 re-election bid"]. ''Reuters'', 24 August 2010.</ref>

===2010–2011 protests and violence===
In April 2010, Wade came under fire for unveiling the [[African Renaissance Monument]], a monument that was deemed too expensive. It was also criticised by religious leaders for [[Modesty#Islam|the immodest attire]] of the women in the monument. While there was domestic criticism, the United States' [[Jesse Jackson]] and Malawian President [[Bingu wa Mutharika]] praised Wade's representation of Africa. Similarly, [[North Korea]], who contributed to the construction of the monument in exchange for a tract of land, congratulated Wade.{{Citation needed|date=March 2012}}

In December 2010, Senegalese troops engaged and repulsed 100 [[Movement of Democratic Forces of Casamance|MFDC]] rebels after they attacked Bignona, [[Casamance]].<ref>[http://www.english.rfi.fr/africa/20101228-senegalese-army-sweeps-casamance-after-fight-separatists Senegalese army sweeps Casamance after fight with separatists] RFI, 28 December 2010</ref> In February 2011, the Senegalese government cut ties with [[Iran]], later alleging that forensic analysis of bullets obtained from rebels revealed that the Iranian government had supplied them.<ref>[https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2011-02-25/senegal-forensic-study-shows-iranian-weapons-used-in-casamance.html Senegal Forensic Study Shows Iranian Weapons Used in Casamance] Bloomberg</ref>

On 18 February 2011, Oumar Bocoum, a soldier, used gasoline to set himself on fire outside the presidential palace in [[Dakar]], following a pattern of protest used [[Arab Spring|throughout the Middle East]].<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20110219024034/http://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5gHLhVr18Pw3zdi037zbzA7Y_4C-g?docId=2077ec6cf99f4510b6d2f77ca1054706 Man dies after setting himself on fire in Senegal] The Associated Press</ref><ref>[https://news.yahoo.com/s/ac/20110224/pl_ac/7929736_benin_senegal_mauritania_impacted_by_protest_movements Benin, Senegal, Mauritania Impacted by Protest Movements] Yahoo!</ref>

In June, after violent [[June 23 Movement in Senegal|protests]], Wade dropped plans for two constitutional changes: [[First-past-the-post voting|lowering the percentage of votes required]] for a first-round victory from 50% to 25% and creating the [[Vice President#In government|position of vice-president]], also to be elected. Critics feared that Wade would use this to ensure his re-election against a split opposition, and to make his son vice-president.<ref>[https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-africa-13887613 "Senegal: Abdoulaye Wade drops poll plans after riots"], BBC News, 23 June 2011.</ref> In response to a protest planned for 23 July, a ban on protesting in Dakar was laid down on 21 July 2011; the protesters in response moved the planned protest outside the city.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-africa-14239692 |title=Senegal's President Abdoulaye Wade bans demonstrations |website=BBC News |date=21 July 2011 |access-date=28 March 2012}}</ref>

===Constitutional Court term limit ruling===
On 27 January 2012, the [[Constitutional Court of Senegal]] ruled that Wade was allowed to run for a third term – according to the ruling, his first term did not count under the new constitution.<ref name="BBC-Wade-run-court">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-africa-16770305|title=Senegal clashes erupt as court clears Wade poll bid|date=28 January 2012|work=BBC News}}</ref>

Protests erupted the following day. Buildings burned across the capital [[Dakar]]. Police fired [[tear gas]] at youth protesters who questioned the ruling. Wade made a television appearance in which he called the protests "displays of petulance" and promised an "open" electoral campaign with "no restrictions on freedom." Protesters said that they would turn the [[Place de l'Obelisque]] in central Dakar into the country's version of [[Tahrir Square]], the focal point of the [[2011 Egyptian revolution]] which led to the ouster of Egyptian President [[Hosni Mubarak]].<ref name="BBC-Wade-run-court"/>

Head of the [[Party of Independence and Labour]] and member of the M23 opposition activist group [[Amath Dansokho]] told reporters, "Abdoulaye Wade has declared war on the people". Truckloads of police in full riot gear and armed with tear gas grenade launchers and truncheons surrounded the presidential palace used by Wade. Leading human rights activist [[:fr:Alioune Tine|Alioune Tine]] was detained.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-senegal-election-idUSTRE80R0LN20120128|title=Senegal opposition urges more "resistance" after riots|date=28 January 2012|work=Reuters}}</ref>

The protests continued into February. Riot police fired volleys of tear gas and rubber bullets in Dakar on 19 February 2012, one week before the election.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.africasia.com/services/news_africa/article.php?ID=CNG.0da26a67d1722e0e7b5d87bdd30d9048.a81|title=Senegal protesters clash with police a week before vote|date=19 February 2012|work=AfricAsia}}{{Dead link|date=September 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

The protests finally ended when Sall defeated Wade in the runoff election.

==Candidates==
In addition to the fourteen eventual candidates, [[Bruno d'Erneville]], President of Programme Action Citoyenne Totale, musician [[Youssou N'Dour]],<ref>{{cite news|title=Youssou N'Dour announces Senegal presidential bid|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-africa-16387629|work=BBC News|date=2 January 2012|access-date=2 January 2012}}</ref> [[Abdourahmane Sarr]] and [[Kéba Keinde]] intended to run in the election as independents. In January 2012, Bruno d'Erneville formed an alliance with [[:fr:Ibrahima Fall (homme politique)|Ibrahima Fall]]
and withdrew.<ref>{{cite news|title=Ibrahima Fall et Bruno d'Erneville mettent en place 'une alliance stratégique'|url=http://www.aps.sn/spip.php?article89431|work=Agence de Presse Sénégalaise|date=19 January 2012|access-date=4 April 2012}}{{Dead link|date=November 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> The other three intended candidates were barred from running in the election over insufficiency of legitimate signatures to endorse their campaigns.<ref name="BBC-Wade-run-court"/><ref name="liste des candidates">{{cite web |url=http://www.rewmi.com/L-integralite-de-l-arret-du-Conseil-constitutionnel-du-27-janvier-2012_a55819.html |title=L'intégralité de l'arrêt du Conseil constitutionnel du 27 janvier 2012|website=Rewmi |access-date=28 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120130004755/http://www.rewmi.com/L-integralite-de-l-arret-du-Conseil-constitutionnel-du-27-janvier-2012_a55819.html |archive-date=30 January 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

